From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lpgauthier <lpgauthier@circworld.qc.ca>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 16:51:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 25/30] Reject paths with NUL bytes in the standard file
 functions (CVE-2006-7243, CVE-2015-2348, CVE-2015-4025)

A NUL byte in a path argument was passed to the C file APIs, which
silently used the truncated path (e.g. "upload.php\0.jpg" acting on
"upload.php"). Every path argument of these functions is now checked
right after parsing: a warning "Path must not contain NUL bytes" and
FALSE, as in later PHP 5 versions:

  fopen, file, file_get_contents, file_put_contents, readfile, copy,
  rename, unlink, mkdir, rmdir, tempnam, realpath, get_meta_tags,
  fnmatch, chdir, opendir/dir, scandir, glob, chmod, chown/chgrp
  (and l*), touch, disk_free_space/disk_total_space, readlink,
  linkinfo, symlink, link, md5_file, sha1_file, iptcembed,
  getimagesize, highlight_file/show_source, php_strip_whitespace,
  parse_ini_file, set_include_path, error_log (file destination),
  is_uploaded_file, move_uploaded_file (CVE-2015-2348)

php_stat() (file_exists, is_file, is_dir, stat, filesize, ...) returns
FALSE without a warning, as upstream does, since these are used as
probes. The check is a macro in main/php.h (PHP_NUL_PATH_CHECK).

Based on CentOS 5 php53 CVE-2006-7243 and Debian squeeze-lts
CVE-2015-2348 / CVE-2015-4025-4026 patches, written for 5.2.17's
argument parsing.

The expectations of the ext/standard/tests/file tests that relied on the
truncation (20 tests, and the 8 matching -win32 variants) are updated
the way upstream did.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
